{"name":"Vermilion Waxcap","hex":"#d46036","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vermilion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vivid red synthetic pigment made of mercury sulfide, cinnabar.","A bright orange-red colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brilliant red colour.","Having the color of the vermilion dye."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vermilion"},{"term":"waxcap","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any agaric mushroom of the family Hygrophoraceae."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/waxcap"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hygrocybe miniata","blocks":[{"text":"Hygrocybe miniata, commonly known as the vermilion waxcap or miniature waxy cap, is a small, bright red or red-orange mushroom of the waxcap genus Hygrocybe. It is a cosmopolitan species, that is found worldwide. In Europe, it is found in fields, on sandy heaths, or grassy clearings in the autumn. In Australia, it is found in rainforest and eucalypt forest as well as heathland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Hygrocybe_miniata_-_Ferndale_Park.jpg/330px-Hygrocybe_miniata_-_Ferndale_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hygrocybe_miniata","wikidata":"Q3270155"},{"title":"Vermilion","blocks":[{"text":"Vermilion is a color family and toxic pigment most often used between antiquity and the 19th century and made from the powdered mineral cinnabar. It is synonymous with red orange."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Lacquerware-roundbox-w-children.jpg/330px-Lacquerware-roundbox-w-children.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vermilion","wikidata":"Q737438"},{"title":"Hygrocybe","blocks":[{"text":"Hygrocybe is a genus of agarics in the family Hygrophoraceae. Called waxcaps in English, basidiocarps are often brightly coloured and have dry to waxy caps, white spores, and smooth, ringless stems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/2011-06-17_Hygrocybe_cantharellus_69428_cropped.jpg/330px-2011-06-17_Hygrocybe_cantharellus_69428_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hygrocybe","wikidata":"Q520530"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Tizian_041.jpg/500px-Tizian_041.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Tizian_041.jpg/500px-Tizian_041.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Venetian painter Titian used vermilion for dramatic effect. In the Assumption of the Virgin (1516–18), the vermilion robes draw the eye to the main characters.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATizian_041.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Cinnabar-172403.jpg/120px-Cinnabar-172403.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Cinnabar-172403.jpg/250px-Cinnabar-172403.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Cinnabar-172403.jpg/120px-Cinnabar-172403.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACinnabar-172403.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Cinnabar-3d6b.jpg/120px-Cinnabar-3d6b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Cinnabar-3d6b.jpg/250px-Cinnabar-3d6b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wiki